<article class="env-lemma" id="055T">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/055T">Lemma <span data-tag="055T">33.25.5</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $d \geq 0$. Let $W \subset \mathbf{A}^ d_ k$ be nonempty open. Then there exists a closed point $w \in W$ such that $k \subset \kappa (w)$ is finite separable. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      After possible shrinking $W$ we may assume that $W = \mathbf{A}^ d_ k \setminus V(f)$ for some $f \in k[x_1, \ldots , x_ d]$. If the lemma is wrong then $f(a_1, \ldots , a_ d) = 0$ for all $(a_1, \ldots , a_ d) \in (k^{sep})^ d$. This is absurd as $k^{sep}$ is an infinite field. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>